good afternoon everyone  marta and i are heartened to see you on  our screens and we're joined today by  secretary and vice president for  university life kimberly  cruz  julia zukovitz a junior in franklin  college and university chaplain sharon  coogler  to everyone online thank you for taking  the time to participate in this vigil  for peace  all of us recognize  that the world is in a somber perilous  place  and over the past few days i've watched  with horror as i know you have  vladimir putin's invasion of ukraine  putin's aggression against a sovereign  independent nation  raises the specter of prolonged conflict  throughout europe  this is the largest ground attack in  europe since world war ii  we know from history that the risks of  miscalculation are dangerously high  and at yale we are compelled by our  common sense of humanity to reject this  violence in the strongest terms  we stand for peace  we grieve for the lives lost  we also support the students and  scholars of the yale ukrainian community  and those yale students and scholars  from elsewhere in the region  including russia who i am confident do  not support this invasion  i know many of our students and  colleagues are shocked and distressed  they fear for the future and for the  safety of loved ones and friends  over the past week yale staff and  faculty have been reaching out to them  to offer support  for example the office of international  students and scholars is working with  each affected student  or helping them with post graduation  plans employment authorizations and  travel needs  we're monitoring developments regarding  immigration options and sharing with  students and scholars relevant updates  for undergraduates from ukraine on  financial aid we will reevaluate the  financial aid awards they receive if  if there are changes  in in your economic  situation  we're advocating for policies that  protect the legal status of ukrainian  students and scholars in the us during  and beyond their studies here  we're supporting consultations with  immigration attorneys for ukrainian  students and scholars who need  assistance in navigating their future  status in the united states  in the days ahead  we will continue to work with individual  students in any way we can  in addition  faculty members on our campus are  helping policymakers think through how  to respond  the coming week the jackson institute  will be hosting an expert panel  discussion  we'll hear from faculty members who have  long studied the history of the region  and can share insights into the current  conflict and anticipate  what may come next  and how we can respond  i very much hope the scholars and  creative thinkers of our community can  help the world chart a path toward peace  right now  the spirit of division around the globe  seems stronger than ever  we must respond by embracing the ways we  are connected  not all of us work or study in fields  that can contribute solutions directly  to global global conflicts of this kind  but all of us play vital roles in  creating an environment of peace  and belonging at yale  and within all communities of which we  are apart  so let us treat one another and  ourselves with kindness and  understanding  let us approach our lives with gratitude  and with mindfulness  during this time of crisis  it gives me hope when i see our  community's commitment to one another  and to peace  please keep in mind  in your minds  the families  and friends who are in the ukraine right  now  with standing  and surviving  and combating  this unjustified barrage  thank you for joining us i'll now tune  turn things over to secretary and vice  president for university life  kimberly goff cruz  you president salovey  i joined the president in welcoming  everyone to this vigil for peace  we are here to support our ukrainian  faculty students staff and alumni as  well as those with ties to ukraine and  to find strength in one another  some of you may have been at the rally  on campus today and i know that there  are other events going on  this week including one today with the  symphony  tonight we create a virtual space to  join people around the world and praying  for an end to war in ukraine and for  peace  we know that events that seem rather  distant  have global effects they can touch  people around us and the people who are  important to them  of course we do not need to have  personal ties to ukraine to feel shock  fear and anger and to wish for peace  some of you here this evening may have  experienced violence or war yourself  we are grateful for your presence and  solidarity  we are profoundly affected by this  week's events because we care for  humanity in ukraine  in the world and at yale  so i encourage all of us to look out for  one another  especially people in your social  communities classes and work settings as  we process what is happening  if you need it  i encourage you to seek support whether  from your school or residential college  mental health and counseling the  chaplain's office our employee  assistance programs or of course from  friends and family  as these events unfold we will support  members of our community from ukraine  russia and the region in the ways that  president salovey described  we've got you we really got you  and we will continue yale's important  work to serve humanity and to make for a  safer healthier more equitable world  before i turn to our next speaker i  would like to share some of martin  luther king jr's remarks when he  received the nobel prize in  1964. he was speaking about the nuclear  arms race  but his words have resonance tonight  he said  so we must fix our vision not merely on  the negative expulsion of war  but upon the positive affirmation of  peace  we must see that peace represents a  sweeter music  a cosmic melody that is far superior to  the discords of war  somehow we must transform the dynamics  of the world's power struggle from the  negative  to a positive contest to harness man's  creative genius for the purpose of  making peace and prosperity a reality  for all of the nations of the world  if we have the will and determination to  mount such peace to such a peace  offensive he said  we will unlock  tightly sealed doors of hope  and transform our imminent cosmic elegy  into a psalm of creative fulfillment  again thank you for joining us  i will now turn to yulia zolkovitz a  junior in yale college and a citizen of  ukraine  hi everyone um thank you bitter  and thank you kimberly for everything  you said um my name is yulia and i'm a  junior in franklin and um i was born and  raised in ukraine um  as president salve and kimberly have  already mentioned  an unthinkable  is happening right now in my home  country in ukraine  on the 24th of february at 5 00 am  putin invaded ukraine invaded my home  country and forever changed my life and  lives of millions of other ukrainians  um i was lucky enough to  not be there when everything started and  not to be there right now and understand  and acknowledge how lucky i am but  millions of people who are still in  ukraine are not that lucky  everyone who is still in the major  cities and in the areas of active  military actions have to spend days and  nights hiding in the bombshell shelters  trying to protect themselves against the  russian missile attacks  however the ukrainian army has been  extremely successful with fighting the  russian forces  we won most of the crown battles and we  continue to stop the russian invasion  and continue to stop them against  progressing further into the country  it is really tough being away from home  and not being able to be there for my  family  but um  i hope as most of you um know there was  a 2pm rally for ukraine on cross campus  and um  by this i'm trying to support my country  support my family and do everything i  can being thousands of miles away i have  received incredible support from the  administration from my fellow students  from um other ukrainians at yale in  yale mad school and yale divinity school  from yale new haven hospital the support  that eel has given me has been  incredible and i cannot imagine a better  place to be right now  i urge all of you to  take a minute today to see to think how  lucky and privileged you are to be away  from the war right now  and how lucky you are to be in the us  um  or wherever you are right now where  you're tuning in um  not to be in the active military zone  where  the war against humanity the war against  one of the oldest nations in europe the  genocide of ukrainian people is  happening right now  thank you all for coming here this means  a lot to me this means a lot to my  family um thank you for tuning in and  i'm gonna  pass it to um sharon cugler the  university chaplain thank you  thank you so much julia you've shown  such grace and strength this last week  and our prayers are with you  my beloved yale family  this is a vigil calling for peace  and my words are intended to both  amplify that call  and turn it into action  our world this beautiful yet imperfect  world that we all share  breaks a little bit more every day we  have been living with a deep burden of  global unrest uncertainty  grief  and fear that is starting to feel  brutally defiant in its staying power  our spirits are suffering under the  stress of it all  and now here we are  gathered in this imperfect way across  these many screens  longing for connection  for reasons to hope  longing for multiple kinds of peace  what can we do thousands of miles away  from a new war  a new invasion to bring peace  this is a vigil  calling for peace  peace in ukraine where people are  fleeing  hiding or preparing to lose their very  lives in defense of their home their way  of life  this is a vigil calling for peace  peace in all places where aggression  poverty  ignorance  and violence oppresses and destroys  our human family  this is a vigil calling for peace  peace in our aching hearts  so that we can find the strength to keep  going and to be part of a kind of  healing that is restorative  this is a vigil calling for peace  peace in our minds  so that we can use them to create a more  just world  where power of means or might is used to  elevate the human condition  and to cherish our planet  this is a vigil calling for peace  peace in our relationships with others  where we can see and be seen  for the uniquely beautiful mysteries  contained  in each of us  this is a vigil calling for peace  peace in the light  peace in the dark peace in the big  peace in the small  peace in the weak  peace in the strong  this is a vigil calling for peace  one of my favorite prayers is also a  hymn  it's the prayer of saint francis  in its entirety it's a distinctly  christian prayer  however the first part feels accessible  to all people and it is in this moment  a much needed bomb for the soul  the prayer asks for us to be instruments  of god's peace  the prayer reveals that making peace  is in our hands  lord make me an instrument of your peace  where there is hatred let me sow love  where there is injury pardon  where there is doubt  faith  where there is despair  hope  where there is darkness  light  where there is sadness  joy  this is a vigil calling for peace  may you be shalom  may you be salam  may you be shanti  may you bring peace  i now invite you to join me in observing  a moment of silence honoring those  across the world particularly in ukraine  who have been lost  those who are grieving and those living  in fear for what might be coming next  focus simply on being still  let your mind turn to what it might mean  to be an instrument  of peace  thank you  this concludes the formal part of this  vigil  i invite you to linger a bit in this  zoom space we have arranged for a couple  of pieces of music to be played to  soothe your soul  let the music bless you  and then please feel free to leave when  you are ready and perhaps seek out a  friend to connect with over dinner this  evening  call a family member just to check in  or introduce yourself to someone new  and be an instrument of peace  thank you so much for joining us today  peace be with you  and god bless the people of ukraine  you

